window.docContents[161].resolve({"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Small-rigorous-improvement":{"contents":"* Question* Context change* Attribute change* Risk shown* PhysLib stage\n* Once A and B are coupled, what bears the total mass and the oscillation frequency?* Open the L1 black boxes into bodies A and B and springs kA, kC, kB; kC joins A and B, so they interact. The coupled pair has its own sort and a nominal PairId independent of its parameters; spring configurations also carry signed extensions.* Per-part mass, stiffness, and displacement; total mass of the pair; normal-mode angular frequency of the pair; effective stiffness.* The pair's mass adds from its bodies, but normal-mode frequency and series stiffness do not; parallel stiffness adds under its own sort-specific license.* Stage 3: sort-specific assembly licenses.\n\nWith B clamped, A is held by its wall spring kA and the coupling spring kC in parallel.\nThe total mass belongs to the pair whole; angular frequency does not add across the bodies,\nand a normal mode is a property of the coupled whole. The parallel stiffness is extensive\nfor the parallel sort, while series stiffness is whole-proper rather than additive. At L3,\neach body is still assumed rigid, homogeneous, and point-like, with its mass on the spring\naxis; an imbalance would make it wobble.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L2 — The pair is a system too","header":"5.3.5. Small rigorous improvement","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Small-rigorous-improvement"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Small-rigorous-improvement":{"contents":"* Question* Context change* Attribute change* Risk shown* PhysLib stage\n* The pair now has bodies, a coupling spring, and multiple records. What must hold, for which systems or objects?* Give the nominal pair IDs P and Q a Sorted instance into pairS, and designate bodies and the coupling spring.* Pair mass, normal-mode frequency, coupling-spring extension, requirement scope, referent, and gate.* An unscoped claim cannot say whether it applies to every pair or to every state of P; a bare real and a non-sort are rejected as scopes.* Stage 4 requirements and audits.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold","header":"5.7.5. Small rigorous improvement","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Small-rigorous-improvement"}});